notes:
DNS (Domain name system) - Attaches names to IP addresses.
Domain hierarchy - Right to left order in the domain name systems.
  Root domain - "." Just the period.
  TLD (Top-level domain) - The most righthand part of a domain name like .com . Splits into two types.
    gTLD (Generic top level) - Used to show what the TLD is for like edu = education, org = organisation.
    ccTLD (Country code top level domain) - Used for geographical purposes, for example .ca for sites based in Canada.
  Second-level domain - The name of the DNS, for example github. Limited to 63 characters and also a-z, 0-9 and hyphens (cannot start or end with hyphens or have consecutive hyphens.)
  Subdomain - Sits on the left-hand side of the Second-level domain, for example admin. .
    Subdomain limits - Same restriction as Second-level domain. Can create multiple subdomains to create longer names 253 character limit.
DNS record types - DNS isn't just for websites.
  A record - Resolve to IPv4 addresses.
  AAAA record - Resolve to IPv6 adresses.
  CNAME record - Resolve to another domain. So it maps an alias (subdomain) to another domain name rather than an IP address.
  MX record - Resolve to the address of the server that handle the email for the domain. So directs emails to the right mail server.
  TXT record - A TXT record lets a domain owner attach arbitrary, human- or machine-readable text to a domain. Originally for notes but now primarily used for security.
DNS request:
  1. When a request is made, your computer will first check its local cache to see if you've previously looked up the adress recently; if not a request to your recurseive DNS server will be made.
  2. Recursive DNS server has a local cache of recently looked up domain names. If a result is found locally, this is sent back to your computer and the request ends here.
     If the request is not found locally, a journey begins to find the correct answer, starting with the internet's root DNS server.
  3. Root servers redirect you to the correct Top Level Domain Server, depending on your request, for example .com .
  4. The TLD server holds records for where to find the authoritative server to answer the DNS request. The authoritative server is often also known as the nameserver.
  5. Depending on the record type, the DNS record is then sent back to the Recursive DNS Server, where a local copy will be cached for future requests.
    
